WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:04.930
Homestar Runner captivated the pre-YouTube
internet with its offbeat characters, its

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.890
simple cartooning, and its...

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:07.380
Strong Bad.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.460
Who are the brothers who created these absurdly
entertaining personas?

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.750
How did the show evolve over time?

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.200
What was the key to its success?

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.300
Homestar Runner started out as a kids book.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.500
Created by brothers Matt and Mike Chapman
and their filmmaker friend Craig Zobel during

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:26.560
the Summer Olympics in 1996, the book was
called The Homestar Runner Enters the Strongest

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.980
Man in the World Contest.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.680
It introduced static versions of such beloved
characters as Homestar Runner, Pom Pom, and

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:33.180
the Cheat.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.760
Strong Bad was there too, playing a straight-up
villain in his premiere role.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:41.340
"Ohhh, these grapes are succulently crushing
my windpipe."

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:45.600
After taking a couple of hours to craft the
characters, they finished the book in a single

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.079
day, showing both a flair for speedy production
and the trademark offbeat comedy that would

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.400
come to define the site.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:55.500
They printed a few copies for their friends,
and eventually made a short animation based

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.620
on Homestar using Mario Paint.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:58.620
"Look out!

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.480
It's strong bad!"

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.720
A few years after the book, the Chapman brothers,
also known as "The Brothers Chaps," began

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.140
to think about the possibilities.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.640
The pair believed in the potential so much
that Mike actually dropped out of grad school

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.500
in order to start teaching himself how to
animate in Flash.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.180
"Whoa man these graphics are state of the
art graphics."

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.020
But, of course, in order to produce a video,
they needed something to put in it, and they

00:01:26.021 --> 00:01:29.229
decided to go back to their old children's
book for inspiration.

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:33.890
Thus it came about that Homestar Runner found
its way off the page and onto the screen.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.369
The cartooning of the book was simple, and
it proved the perfect subject to use for animation

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:38.860
practice.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:43.180
The website launched in 2000, initially serving
simply as a place to house some of the videos

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.180
they were creating.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.320
One of the oddest things about Homestar Runner
is the name itself.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.240
With no obvious connection to anything collectively
recognizable, where exactly did this peculiar

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.180
name come from?

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.759
Believe it or not, it originated from an inside
joke that came about when a group of kids

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.720
innocently made fun of a bad commercial.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.790
Mike Chapman explained that the name found
its origin when one day the brothers were

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:08.420
hanging out with their friend James Huggins,
who went on to become a member of the band

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:09.420
Of Montreal.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.180
"Tell them his other significance real quick.”

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.980
"He was the one that coined the phrase ‘Homestar
Runner.’”

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.620
When James was trying to make fun of a commercial
that starred Mark Lemke of the Atlanta Braves,

00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:23.239
he tried to use terminology that was simply
out of his experience, blurting out something

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.520
along the lines of "Homestar runner for the
Braves Mike Lemke!"

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.550
The name obviously stuck in the minds of the
Brothers Chaps, and they tucked it away for

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:31.540
later use.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.060
One bothersome question that has always stood
front and center with Homestar Runner is the

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:38.810
lead character's puzzling lack of arms.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.010
Most of the other characters surrounding Homestar
have arms, even if many of them are missing

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.620
hands or perpetually wearing boxing gloves.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:50.020
But while the lack of arms has never slowed
Homestar Runner down, the question still stands

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:50.660
- why?

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:53.760
The answer, in this case, is right in the
name itself.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.170
As Mike Chapman pointed out, Homestar was
created to be an excellent athlete - not just

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.250
an athlete, but a runner in particular.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.620
Since he was designed to run, why would he
need arms?

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:08.120
Strong Bad, Strong Mad, and Strong Sad make
up the Strong Brothers Three, with the trio

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:11.930
showing up in numerous videos and always causing
some kind of havoc and humor.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.439
But while the characters have their own extremely
unique qualities within the Homestar Runner-verse,

00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:18.819
they aren't actually made up from scratch.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:23.029
It turns out that the entire idea of the "Strong
Clan" is actually borrowed from a team of

00:03:23.029 --> 00:03:26.720
wrestlers that appeared on the '80s NES game
Tag Team Wrestling.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.680
The game follows two different wrestling tag-teams,
the Ricky Fighters and the Strong Bads, with

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.939
one of the latter wearing a red Luchador mask
that is clearly the inspiration for the beloved

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:36.940
Homestar character.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:42.200
It was just a matter of time before the ever-depressing
Strong Sad and the childishly angry Strong

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:47.189
Mad arrived on the scene, and the whole Strong
brotherhood was officially born into existence.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.600
"Finally my comeuppance has arrived."

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:54.840
For a while, the Homestar Runner website clicked
along steadily with a small audience, but

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.960
eventually, the Chapmans realized they were
making it big - all thanks to Strong Bad's

00:03:58.960 --> 00:03:59.460
emails.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.620
The endless procession of shorts that showcased
Strong Bad answering bizarre emails with sometimes

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:08.900
nonsensical answers proved to be the pivotal
turning point that turned Homestar Runner

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.590
into an internet sensation.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:14.209
The best part about the Strong Bad emails,
though, is that they were genuine emails from

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:15.209
real fans.

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:17.579
"Yours truly, Johnny well on.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.419
Oh, I left my Johnny well on.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:23.460
Johnny Well… off."

00:04:24.509 --> 00:04:27.800
While the Chapman brothers have been at the
heart of all things Homestar Runner over the

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.240
years, they probably never could have pulled
off what they did without a little bit of

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:31.740
help.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.740
This came, in particular, from their parents,
who were supportive of their endeavors right

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:36.740
from the get-go.

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.379
Their parents also allowed the brothers to
fill their home with the burgeoning business

00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:44.291
as things like production and fulfillment
of merch orders steadily claimed more and

00:04:44.291 --> 00:04:45.810
more room throughout the house.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.379
Their father worked alongside his sons to
serve as their accountant and to help them

00:04:49.379 --> 00:04:51.430
manage the copyrights for their characters.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:56.530
He was even a driving force behind convincing
Matt to quit his job in 2003 in order to pursue

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:57.840
Homestar full-time.

00:04:58.289 --> 00:05:02.060
While many parents have supported their children's
dreams over the years, there's little doubt

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:04.800
that the Chapmans' folks were more supportive
than most.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.419
When you hear of a site that's getting hundreds
of thousands of visitors, it's only natural

00:05:09.419 --> 00:05:12.050
to wonder how the webmasters have monetized
that traffic.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:15.599
But, while the Chapman brothers did, indeed,
make a living off of Homestar Runner for the

00:05:15.599 --> 00:05:20.030
decade or so that its star was on the rise,
the success was shockingly stripped of any

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:22.659
of the ads that typically help bring in the
cash.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:23.860
How did they make their money?

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:24.740
With merch.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:29.280
At times they literally had fans clamoring
for shirts, hoodies, and other merchandise

00:05:29.289 --> 00:05:31.979
emblazoned with their favorite online heroes.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.220
It was the sale of Homestar Runner products
that kept that the site alive for years.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:40.460
Of course, you can't mention Strong Bad and
his email success without a shout-out to his

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.180
favorite '80s band, "Limozeen."

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.400
Not only did the band appear in shorts like
the one about crayon color names, but it actually

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.699
manifested in a coloring book that you can
still download and print off for free.

00:05:50.699 --> 00:05:55.439
That's right, Homestar Runner wasn't just
on the forefront of '80s nostalgia and internet

00:05:55.439 --> 00:05:58.819
animation, it also helped pioneer the adult
coloring book genre!

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:01.140
The band's history goes even further than
that, though.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:06.240
Remember how the Chapmans' friend James Huggins,
accidental creator of the name Homestar Runner,

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.009
ended up in the band Of Montreal?

00:06:08.009 --> 00:06:11.870
It turns out that Strong Bad's favorite band
actually opened for them, as well.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:14.920
In 2008, the band played at the Tabernacle
in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:19.860
Ten years after opening the site, the brothers
decided a hiatus was in order.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.220
But that didn't mean that Homestar and company
were being entirely left in the rear-view

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:23.720
mirror.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:28.500
A few years later, they began producing occasional
shorts in order to give their die-hard fans

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.500
some fresh material.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:33.580
But the Brothers Chaps had spent too much
time in the limelight to quietly disappear

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:34.760
into a "normal" life.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:39.500
Instead, they managed to leverage their popularity
as the creators of a cultural phenomenon into

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:44.710
very decent jobs working on shows like Yo
Gabba Gabba! on Nickelodeon and Gravity Falls

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:46.440
and Two More Eggs for Disney.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:50.430
While we still do get the occasional fresh
piece of content, it seems that the days of

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:55.580
weekly Strong Bad emails, the ongoing adventures
of Dangeresque, and Trogdor burninating new

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:58.640
countrysides may be officially part of the
past.

